<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Java War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1825">1825</a>–<a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Diponegoro War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> War was a rebellion in central Java from 1825 to 1830 led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diponegoro">Prince Diponegoro</a> of Yogyakarta against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonial rule and the Javanese courts that cooperated with it. Fuelled by peasant grievances, Islamic sentiment and Dutch interference, it was fought as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war. Diponegoro was captured during negotiations in 1830 and exiled. About 200,000 Javanese died, and the costs led the Dutch to introduce the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultivation-system">Cultivation System</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/java-war.jpg" alt="Java War" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J.P. van de Veer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bestorming_van_Pleret.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-giyanti">Treaty of Giyanti</a> of 1755 divided the Mataram Sultanate into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of Yogyakarta and Surakarta, and the Mangkunegaran was formed in 1757. The weakness of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in the late 18th century left the princely states with de facto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diponegoro">Prince Diponegoro</a>, born in 1785, was the eldest son of Sultan Hamengkubuwono III of Yogyakarta, and through his mother he claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wali-songo">Wali Sanga</a>, the early apostles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. By 1824 several of his allies had been expelled from court, and in early 1825 the Dutch annexed territory from the Sultanate as a lease without consulting him.</p><p>On 17 June 1825 the Dutch Resident Smissaert ordered a road to be repaired that passed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diponegoro">Diponegoro</a>'s estate at Tegalreja. Fights broke out between the road workers and his men, and on 20 July a detachment was sent to arrest him. He withdrew to Selarong Cave and declared his rebellion on 21 July 1825.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Hendrik Merkus de Kock commanded about 12,500 Dutch Indies troops, half of them native recruits whose loyalty the Dutch doubted, and later received 3,154 European soldiers and over 10,000 auxiliaries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diponegoro">Diponegoro</a>'s followers included seventy members of the royal family, and he divided his forces under sixteen commanders of noble birth. The princely courts were split. Rebel forces besieged Yogyakarta until a Dutch relief force arrived, and the war became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> struggle in which disease, hostile villagers and manpower shortages hampered the Dutch. By April 1827 some 1,600 of de Kock's men had been killed.</p><p>From 1827 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> built a system of field fortifications, the Benteng-stelsel, of which 133 of 258 forts were built in 1828 and 1829. Kyai Maja, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diponegoro">Diponegoro</a>'s spiritual adviser, surrendered in November 1828, and Sentot Prawirodirdjo surrendered in October 1829. Diponegoro was left with about fifty men. On 28 March 1830, during negotiations at Magelang, de Kock announced his arrest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diponegoro">Diponegoro</a> was exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manado">Manado</a>, moved to Makassar in 1833 and died on 8 January 1855. At least 200,000 Javanese were killed, with some 15,000 dead on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> side and about 20,000 among the rebels. One third of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>'s population of 6 million was directly affected and about a quarter of its arable land was damaged. The war was the last significant armed resistance to Dutch rule until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-revolution">Indonesian National Revolution</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> lost much of their remaining power. Diponegoro wrote the Babad Diponegoro in exile, and he was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-hero-of-indonesia">National Hero of Indonesia</a> in 1973.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Java War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dutch authority remained limited outside Java, and on Java itself it was challenged by Prince Diponegoro of Yogyakarta, whose rebellion of 1825–1830, the <b>Java War</b>, cost perhaps 200,000 Javanese lives before he was arrested during negotiations and exiled to Manado and later Makassar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>British troops sacked the kraton in 1812, and the Yogyakarta prince Diponegoro led the <b>Java War</b> against the Dutch from 1825 to 1830.</blockquote><a href="/state/yogyakarta-sultanate">Yogyakarta Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
